// ═══ start() — creates NEW thread ═══
Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Running on: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
});
t.start();
// Output: "Running on: Thread-0"  ← NEW thread!
// Main thread continues immediately — parallel execution!

// ═══ run() — NO new thread! ═══
t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Running on: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
});
t.run();  // ← just a method call!
// Output: "Running on: main"  ← SAME thread! No parallelism!
// Main thread is BLOCKED until run() finishes — sequential!

// ═══ WHAT start() DOES INTERNALLY ═══
// 1. Checks thread state (must be NEW → else IllegalThreadStateException)
// 2. Adds thread to ThreadGroup
// 3. Calls native start0() → OS creates a new thread
// 4. The new OS thread calls this.run()
// 5. Caller returns immediately — doesn't wait for run() to finish

// ═══ start() CANNOT BE CALLED TWICE ═══
Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> System.out.println("Hello"));
t2.start();  // OK — state: NEW → RUNNABLE
// t2.start();  // IllegalThreadStateException! state is RUNNABLE or TERMINATED

// ═══ PROOF: different thread names ═══
Runnable task = () -> System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());

System.out.println("Main: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());  // "main"
new Thread(task).start();  // "Thread-0" — different thread!
new Thread(task).run();    // "main" — same thread! No new thread!
```

